WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.220
Two cellular floor plans.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:12.080
A prokaryote, a bacterium, is an open plan: no internal membranes, no true nucleus,

3
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.060
everything happening in one room.

4
00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:19.980
A eukaryote, your cells, is a building of walled offices: a membrane-bound nucleus,

5
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.760
mitochondria, ER, Golgi, lysosomes, each doing one job.

6
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.540
The banner says it: compartments enable specialization.

7
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.720
That single architectural difference is the reason your cells can do things bacteria

8
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:35.720
cannot.

9
00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:40.953
The plasma membrane is the cell's outer wall, and it's a phospholipid bilayer.

10
00:00:41.673 --> 00:00:46.093
Hydrophilic heads face the water on both sides; the hydrophobic core in the middle

11
00:00:46.093 --> 00:00:48.073
blocks most molecules from crossing.

12
00:00:48.653 --> 00:00:53.613
That makes the membrane a selective barrier: impermeable on its own, but studded with

13
00:00:53.613 --> 00:00:55.313
proteins that choose what passes.

14
00:00:56.373 --> 00:01:01.433
Channel proteins open passages for specific ions; receptor proteins receive signals

15
00:01:01.433 --> 00:01:05.593
from outside. The membrane decides what gets in and what stays out.

16
00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:10.567
Two ways the cell moves cargo across that barrier, and a way we proved it.

17
00:01:11.127 --> 00:01:17.287
On the left, a clinical correlate: in familial hypercholesterolemia, broken LDL

18
00:01:17.287 --> 00:01:22.727
receptor endocytosis leaves cholesterol piling up in the arteries. Endocytosis brings

19
00:01:22.727 --> 00:01:29.067
material in. On the right, the experiment that mapped the path out: George Palade's

20
00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:35.287
pulse chase. Tag fresh protein with a brief radioactive pulse, chase with unlabeled,

21
00:01:35.407 --> 00:01:40.787
and watch the labeled cohort travel from rough ER to Golgi to secretion in real

22
00:01:40.787 --> 00:01:43.787
time. That's how we know the secretory pathway is real.

23
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.360
Here's the chapter's whole argument in one disease.

24
00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:53.680
The lysosome is the cell's recycling center, full of digestive enzymes sealed safely

25
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:54.680
behind a membrane.

26
00:01:55.340 --> 00:02:00.120
In Tay-Sachs, one of those enzymes, hexosaminidase A, is missing.

27
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:06.400
Without it, a fatty molecule called GM2 ganglioside can't be broken down, so it

28
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:12.360
accumulates, the lysosome swells, and the cell, especially nerve cells, chokes:

29
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:17.280
muscle weakness, neurological decline, death usually before age 3.

30
00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:21.220
One enzyme, one compartment, one child's life.

31
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.320
When a compartment fails, everything it was holding back comes apart.
